1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:06,130
This video shows you how to give values a type in a Code step,

2
00:00:06,130 --> 00:00:09,350
and how to convert a value to another type.

3
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:13,957
This course starts from a pipeline that reads Kubernetes container logs.

4
00:00:13,957 --> 00:00:17,470
Its Parser step reads the envelope around each line.

5
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:19,949
The app's own line is in log.

6
00:00:19,949 --> 00:00:22,390
Most apps write it as JSON.

7
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:24,848
Add a Code step to read it.

8
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:31,292
The json function reads text into an object.

9
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:33,197
This doesn't compile.

10
00:00:33,197 --> 00:00:34,490
log is a variant.

11
00:00:34,490 --> 00:00:41,230
The Parser step declared no fields, so the type of log is known only record by record.

12
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:43,148
Give it a type first.

13
00:00:43,148 --> 00:00:44,790
log is a string.

14
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:47,617
Now app holds the fields of the line.

15
00:00:47,617 --> 00:00:49,990
Each one is still undeclared.

16
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:54,830
Next, mark the slow requests: those that took over a second.

17
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:57,790
The fields of app are variants too.

18
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:01,312
Name the fields you use in the type of app.

19
00:01:01,312 --> 00:01:07,670
msg is a string, status an integer, and duration_ms a floating-point number.

20
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:10,870
The three dots keep the fields you didn't name.

21
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:13,940
Now each named field has its type.

22
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:18,790
This request took 73 milliseconds, so slow is false.

23
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:22,874
Now the major version of each app, as a number.

24
00:01:22,874 --> 00:01:26,870
Its version label holds text such as 1.42.0.

25
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:30,370
as converts a value to another type.

26
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:33,172
This fails on every record.

27
00:01:33,172 --> 00:01:36,823
1.42.0 is not an integer, so major is null,

28
00:01:36,823 --> 00:01:42,030
and each record is marked with a CoercionRefused exception.

29
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:45,830
Convert only the part before the first dot.

30
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:48,630
major is now the integer 1.

31
00:01:48,680 --> 00:01:50,337
Two records still fail.

32
00:01:50,337 --> 00:01:53,147
Their lines are stack traces, not JSON.

33
00:01:53,147 --> 00:01:55,430
The third video handles them.

34
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:57,803
Save on a new branch.

35
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:04,184
Check on learning.

36
00:02:04,384 --> 00:02:09,400
A Parser step declared no fields, and your code calls json on one of them.

37
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:11,559
Why doesn't it compile?

38
00:02:15,959 --> 00:02:18,311
The field is a variant.

39
00:02:18,311 --> 00:02:23,307
Give it a type first, as in log: str = log.

40
00:02:24,207 --> 00:02:28,098
What does as give when a value can't convert?

41
00:02:32,498 --> 00:02:37,224
Null, and the record is marked with a CoercionRefused exception.

42
00:02:38,124 --> 00:02:41,491
Next, you handle values that are missing.
